  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up loss bowl with simulated mini-batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.3 × loss. It is 4 times steeper in x, so a learning rate above 0.5 overshoots and diverges in x. The z direction never diverges in this range.",
    "Step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with sd 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for each setting: 2 at batch 1, exactly 0 at batch 64. It comes from a fixed seed, and a new run continues the sequence.",
    "Arrows are 0.15 × the slope size. Orange: minus the noisy mini-batch slope, which is the way the ball moves. Grey: minus the true slope. The readout shows the mini-batch slope size, unscaled.",
    "A run stops after 40 steps (finished), or when the loss goes above 8 (diverged), which means the ball has left the drawn bowl. The ball and trail are capped at the bowl's edge, but Loss keeps its true value.",
    "With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average. Single steps can raise it, especially with small batches."
  ]
}